#X obj 10 706 cnv 1 1260 190 empty empty TRY\ THIS 8 12 0 13 #e6f6e9 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 732 1) Turn the volume up. With tanh \, raise the drive from 0 to 8: clean sine -> warm saturation -> almost a square. Watch the orange bar reach the flat ends of the curve., f 86;
#X text 20 783 2) gauss: the pitch jumps an octave. relu: half the wave disappears (half-wave rectifier). sin + high drive: a wavefolder \, the West Coast synthesis sound., f 86;
#X text 20 819 3) Move the offset (bias) with tanh: an asymmetric operating point adds even harmonics - this is how a biased tube or transistor stage colours sound., f 86;
#X text 640 732 4) Draw a curve with several bumps: every wiggle multiplies the harmonics. Fill with "noise" for a harsh \, digital sound., f 88;
#X text 640 768 5) Think like a neuron: drive = the weights \, offset = the bias \, the curve = the activation. Lesson 3 puts all three into one object., f 88;
#X text 640 804 IN PD: [nn.act~] is a lookup table read by [tabread4~] - open it. The drawing on the right uses exactly the same technique., f 88;
